															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/uncannyon" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4374851); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=4374851&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606121034" alt="Uncannyon" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=4374851)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/uncannyon" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4374851); return false;" >Uncannyon</a></span> <span class="odate time_1579581285 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">21 Jan 2020 04:34</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4474850">
									

<p>SCP K contests have a habit of inspiring some of the most profound, thought provoking, and exploitatively creative pieces of fiction on the site – so of course I got hooked on the idea of an affectionate parody of golden age murder mystery tropes.</p>
<p>I do hope that you enjoy it!</p>
<p>My grateful thanks to the lovely folk in #TheCritters for their kindness, support, and for managing to still somehow find typos after eight revisions of this skip. Doubtless, there are still a few more patiently awaiting detection.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">◆&nbsp;My&nbsp;Slot&nbsp;Preferences&nbsp;◆</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">◇&nbsp;My&nbsp;Slot&nbsp;Preferences&nbsp;◇</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<ul>
<li>5005</li>
<li>5002</li>
<li>5003</li>
<li>5010</li>
<li>5020</li>
</ul>
<p>And after that whatever’s lowest.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">◆&nbsp;Image&nbsp;credit&nbsp;◆</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">◇&nbsp;Image&nbsp;credit&nbsp;goes&nbsp;to:&nbsp;◇</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><span style="text-decoration: line-through;">&quot;Smile doesn't get old&quot; by Ilya Nodia, Irina Muravyova is licensed under CC BY-NC-ND 4.0<br />
<a href="https://search.creativecommons.org/photos/308a8095-e7d8-404f-b6bd-9b690592c640">https://search.creativecommons.org/photos/308a8095-e7d8-404f-b6bd-9b690592c640</a></span></p>
<p><span style="text-decoration: line-through;">&quot;An Old Girl&quot; by Gustav<br />
<a href="https://www.flickr.com/photos/gustavm/4614129944/">https://www.flickr.com/photos/gustavm/4614129944/</a></span></p>
<p>&quot;The Rest&quot; by Bernhard Frank<br />
<a href="https://www.flickr.com/photos/bernhardfrank/20821539504/">https://www.flickr.com/photos/bernhardfrank/20821539504/</a></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">◆&nbsp;All&nbsp;of&nbsp;the&nbsp;References&nbsp;◆</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">◇&nbsp;All&nbsp;of&nbsp;the&nbsp;References&nbsp;◇</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Needless to say, <strong>Murder, She Redacted</strong> is largely a tongue-in-cheek parody of classic cosy, cheesy, campy crime and mystery stories, especially the Miss Marple books and the Murder, She Wrote TV series. A completely pointless list of every specific reference is included below, for reasons I cannot fully explain:</p>
<ul>
<li>The title is a reference to both the <em>Murder, She Said</em> Marple story and the <em>Murder, She Wrote</em> TV series.</li>
<li>Director Angela Child is a reference to <em>Murder, She Wrote</em> star Angela Lansbury and crime author Lee Child.</li>
<li>Research Head John Creek is a reference to author John le Carré and Jonathan Creek from the TV series of the same name.</li>
<li>Special Residential Unity 221B is a reference to the residence of Arthur Conan Doyle’s Sherlock Holmes.</li>
<li>The entity’s lack of anus and sex organs is a reference to the unthreatening old lady detective archetype being “pure” and sexless.</li>
<li>5000-A events are a reference to the absurd numbers of murders amateur sleuths just happen to run into.</li>
<li>5000-B instances are a reference to the ridiculous number of relatives amateur sleuths tend to have in order to justify their involvement in so many cases.</li>
<li>Copper Nitrate is a pun on what the police get paid after dark.</li>
<li>The 34 homicide investigations references the 34 Miss Marple stories.</li>
<li>The 264 homicides references the number of <em>Murder, She Wrote</em> episodes.</li>
<li>30/09/1984 references the airing date of the first episode of <em>Murder, She Wrote</em>.</li>
<li>Police Sergeant Lewis Foyle-Brown references Lewis, from the TV series of the same name, Foyle, from the TV series <em>Foyle’s War</em>, and Father Brown, from the short stories by G. K. Chesterton</li>
<li>Field Agent Frank Lynley references Frank Colombo from the TV series <em>Colombo</em>, and Thomas Lynley from <em>The Inspector Lynley Mysteries</em> TV series.</li>
<li>James Butler is a reference to crime author P. D. James and the “The Butler Did It” trope.</li>
<li>St. Mary’s Church is a reference St. Mary Mead, fictional home of Miss Marple.</li>
<li>Kembleford is the fictional Cotswold village of Father Brown.</li>
<li>HMP Farnleigh is the fictional prison that appears in the TV series <em>Morse</em> and <em>Endeavour</em>.</li>
<li>Field Agent Vera Cadfael is a reference to DCI Vera Stanhope from the TV series <em>Vera</em> and Brother Cadfael from the mystery series by Edith Pargeter.</li>
<li>Peter Sayers is a reference to the Lord Peter Wimsey series and its author Dorothy L. Sayers.</li>
<li>The Earl of Midsummer is a reference to the fictional English county of Midsummer, setting of the <em>Midsummer Murders</em> TV series.</li>
<li>Patrick is a reference to Patrick Jane of the TV series <em>The Mentalist</em>.</li>
<li>Paul is a reference to Paul Temple from the mystery series by Francis Durbridge.</li>
</ul>
<p>*Rosemary and Laura are a reference to the main characters of the <em>Rosemary &amp; Thyme</em> TV series.</p>
<ul>
<li>Doctor Adrian Banks is a reference to Adrian Monk, from the TV series <em>Monk</em>, and DCI Banks, from the TV series of the same name.</li>
<li>Dr. Sloan is a reference to the character of the same name from TV series <em>Diagnosis, Murder</em>.</li>
<li>Dr. Blake is a reference to the character of the same name from the TV series <em>The Doctor Blake Mysteries</em>.</li>
<li>Dr. Frost is a reference to DI Jack Frost from the TV series <em>A Touch of Frost</em>.</li>
<li>Security officer Wainthropp is a reference to Hetty Wainthropp from the TV series <em>Hetty Wainthropp investigates</em>.</li>
<li>Dr. Wright is a reference to Phoenix Wright from the <em>Ace Attorney</em> video game series.</li>
<li>Joe Argyle is a reference to Joe Miller, the murderer in TV series <em>Broadchurch</em>, and Jacko Argyle, the murderer’s collaborator in Agatha Christie’s <em>Ordeal By Innocence</em>.</li>
<li>D-890976 is a reference to Agatha Christie’s birth in 1890 and death in 1976.</li>
<li>Senior researcher Rendell is a reference to mystery writer Ruth Rendell.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
